//
// Found while fixing loft#1698 (D-tup-19).  A vector CONSTANT is pre-built from its
// initialiser's literal field writes, and a member of a TUPLE element that is a record or a
// collection is written by a copy those writes never mention — so it read back EMPTY: a
// struct member's fields as 0 and a vector member as `[]`, on both backends with no
// diagnostic.  A struct element with a nested record was already refused for exactly that
// reason; the tuple spelling asked nothing.  It is refused the same way now, pointing at the
// zero-argument function that builds it at run time: a constant holds what its literal writes
// describe.
//
// @falsified-at: 7fb81e270 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret expectations FAIL/1 -> 0/1 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches), native exit 1 -> 0, native expectations FAIL/1 -> 0/1 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches)
// @falsified-by: tests/falsified/1698c-a-constant-of-tuples-with-a-record-member-is-refused.patch — the same channels.
// CHANNEL: the MESSAGE — the control tree compiled this and printed `0 0 202`.
// ARMED: every run.
// WITNESS: the refusal below.
// HOLDS: a flat tuple constant (`vector<(integer, text)>`) still builds; guarded by the
//   constant corpus.

// @EXPECT_ERROR: has a nested record in tuple member 0, which a constant cannot hold
struct Pair { id: integer, tag: integer }
TABLE: vector<(Pair, integer)> = [(Pair { id: 10, tag: 1 }, 101), (Pair { id: 20, tag: 2 }, 202)];
fn main() {
  println("{TABLE[0].0.id} {TABLE[1].0.tag} {TABLE[1].1}");
}
